1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,925
Sweetheart, you know that coffee place you

2
00:00:02,925 --> 00:00:05,363
like, where they write your name on

3
00:00:05,363 --> 00:00:07,801
the cup and it costs seven dollars?

4
00:00:07,981 --> 00:00:10,196
Let me tell you about the place

5
00:00:10,196 --> 00:00:12,196
I liked when I was thirteen.

6
00:00:12,376 --> 00:00:14,628
It was nineteen fifty-seven.

7
00:00:14,808 --> 00:00:17,225
It was on the corner of Main

8
00:00:17,225 --> 00:00:18,951
Street in Mansfield,

9
00:00:18,951 --> 00:00:19,382
Ohio.

10
00:00:19,562 --> 00:00:20,760
And for a nickel,

11
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:22,592
a boy in a white paper hat

12
00:00:22,592 --> 00:00:24,917
would make you the best thing you

13
00:00:24,917 --> 00:00:25,762
ever tasted.

14
00:00:25,942 --> 00:00:28,160
Come with me to the soda fountain.

15
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:31,296
Every town in America had one,

16
00:00:31,296 --> 00:00:31,853
honey.

17
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:34,146
Sometimes three or four.

18
00:00:34,326 --> 00:00:36,159
It was inside the drugstore,

19
00:00:36,159 --> 00:00:37,991
and it was the center of the

20
00:00:37,991 --> 00:00:40,282
whole world if you were a teenager.

21
00:00:40,462 --> 00:00:42,575
Today they're almost all gone.

22
00:00:42,755 --> 00:00:44,687
So I'm going to walk you through

23
00:00:44,687 --> 00:00:46,498
it, from the front door to the

24
00:00:46,498 --> 00:00:49,034
back counter, and tell you what everything

25
00:00:49,034 --> 00:00:50,905
cost and who was sitting where.

26
00:00:51,256 --> 00:00:53,849
Our drugstore was called Hoffman's,

27
00:00:53,849 --> 00:00:55,923
and it had a big glass front

28
00:00:55,923 --> 00:00:57,257
with gold letters,

29
00:00:57,257 --> 00:00:59,405
and a bell over the door that

30
00:00:59,405 --> 00:00:59,998
jingled.

31
00:01:00,178 --> 00:01:02,585
The first thing you saw was the

32
00:01:02,585 --> 00:01:03,672
magazine rack.

33
00:01:03,852 --> 00:01:06,932
Life, Look, the Saturday Evening Post with

34
00:01:06,932 --> 00:01:09,205
a Norman Rockwell on the cover.

35
00:01:09,385 --> 00:01:12,848
Photoplay and Modern Screen for the movie

36
00:01:12,848 --> 00:01:13,355
stars.

37
00:01:13,535 --> 00:01:15,096
And the comic books,

38
00:01:15,096 --> 00:01:17,438
a whole spinning rack of them,

39
00:01:17,438 --> 00:01:18,609
ten cents each.

40
00:01:18,959 --> 00:01:19,771
Mr.

41
00:01:19,951 --> 00:01:22,532
Hoffman didn't mind if you read them

42
00:01:22,532 --> 00:01:24,969
standing up, as long as you didn't

43
00:01:24,969 --> 00:01:26,116
bend the covers.

44
00:01:26,296 --> 00:01:29,452
Danny read every Superman and every Donald

45
00:01:29,452 --> 00:01:31,931
Duck in Ohio standing in front of

46
00:01:31,931 --> 00:01:34,261
that rack and never bought one.

47
00:01:34,441 --> 00:01:35,230
Mr.

48
00:01:35,410 --> 00:01:36,745
Hoffman knew.

49
00:01:36,925 --> 00:01:38,549
He'd say, Danny Miller,

50
00:01:38,549 --> 00:01:41,231
that's the third time you've read that

51
00:01:41,231 --> 00:01:42,219
one this week.

52
00:01:42,399 --> 00:01:44,150
And Danny would say,

53
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:45,639
I'm studying, Mr.

54
00:01:45,819 --> 00:01:46,654
Hoffman.

55
00:01:47,004 --> 00:01:48,925
Next to the door there was a

56
00:01:48,925 --> 00:01:50,023
big penny scale.

57
00:01:50,203 --> 00:01:51,357
You stood on it,

58
00:01:51,357 --> 00:01:52,439
put in a penny,

59
00:01:52,439 --> 00:01:54,531
and a card came out with your

60
00:01:54,531 --> 00:01:57,056
weight on one side and your fortune

61
00:01:57,056 --> 00:01:57,993
on the other.

62
00:01:58,173 --> 00:02:00,820
You will meet a tall dark stranger.

63
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,957
Linda and I weighed ourselves every single

64
00:02:03,957 --> 00:02:05,153
day after school,

65
00:02:05,153 --> 00:02:07,265
and every single day she got a

66
00:02:07,265 --> 00:02:08,884
better fortune than me.

67
00:02:09,064 --> 00:02:11,397
I'm still a little bitter about it.

68
00:02:11,747 --> 00:02:13,205
And in the summer,

69
00:02:13,205 --> 00:02:14,743
there was the sign.

70
00:02:14,923 --> 00:02:17,120
A big blue banner over the door

71
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:19,671
with icicles painted on the letters.

72
00:02:19,851 --> 00:02:21,059
Air Conditioned.

73
00:02:21,239 --> 00:02:22,562
Cool Inside.

74
00:02:22,742 --> 00:02:25,992
In nineteen fifty-seven almost nobody had air

75
00:02:25,992 --> 00:02:27,509
conditioning at home,

76
00:02:27,509 --> 00:02:28,303
sweetheart.

77
00:02:28,483 --> 00:02:30,841
So on the hottest days of July,

78
00:02:30,841 --> 00:02:33,503
half of Mansfield found a reason to

79
00:02:33,503 --> 00:02:34,416
buy aspirin.

80
00:02:34,596 --> 00:02:36,662
You'd walk in from the street and

81
00:02:36,662 --> 00:02:38,665
the cold hit you like jumping in

82
00:02:38,665 --> 00:02:39,229
the lake.

83
00:02:39,409 --> 00:02:41,601
People stood by the door just to

84
00:02:41,601 --> 00:02:42,149
breathe.

85
00:02:42,499 --> 00:02:45,378
In the back was the real drugstore.

86
00:02:45,558 --> 00:02:46,405
Mr.

87
00:02:46,585 --> 00:02:48,280
Hoffman in a white coat,

88
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:49,834
behind a high counter,

89
00:02:49,834 --> 00:02:52,305
with glass jars of colored water in

90
00:02:52,305 --> 00:02:55,130
the window and a hundred little drawers.

91
00:02:55,310 --> 00:02:57,704
He'd been there since before my mother

92
00:02:57,704 --> 00:02:58,271
was born.

93
00:02:58,451 --> 00:03:01,302
He mixed some of the medicines himself,

94
00:03:01,302 --> 00:03:03,130
with a mortar and pestle.

95
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:04,880
And if you were sick,

96
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:07,199
you didn't call a doctor first.

97
00:03:07,379 --> 00:03:09,144
You asked Mr.

98
00:03:09,324 --> 00:03:10,160
Hoffman.

99
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:13,168
He sold everything back there.

100
00:03:13,348 --> 00:03:16,160
Aspirin, Vicks, Ipana toothpaste,

101
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:18,119
Brylcreem for the boys,

102
00:03:18,119 --> 00:03:21,101
Toni home permanents for the girls,

103
00:03:21,101 --> 00:03:23,231
Evening in Paris perfume,

104
00:03:23,231 --> 00:03:25,701
film for your Brownie camera.

105
00:03:25,881 --> 00:03:28,302
You dropped off the film and came

106
00:03:28,302 --> 00:03:30,723
back in a week for your pictures,

107
00:03:30,723 --> 00:03:32,336
with white wavy edges.

108
00:03:32,516 --> 00:03:34,792
And he knew everybody's business.

109
00:03:34,972 --> 00:03:36,980
Every prescription in town.

110
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:40,179
He never said one word to anyone.

111
00:03:40,359 --> 00:03:42,101
That was his religion.

112
00:03:42,451 --> 00:03:44,193
But the soda fountain,

113
00:03:44,193 --> 00:03:44,668
honey.

114
00:03:44,848 --> 00:03:47,553
That was along the whole left wall.

115
00:03:47,733 --> 00:03:49,477
A long marble counter,

116
00:03:49,477 --> 00:03:51,695
cool to your arms in August,

117
00:03:51,695 --> 00:03:54,548
with chrome stools that spun around,

118
00:03:54,548 --> 00:03:56,371
covered in red leather.

119
00:03:56,551 --> 00:03:58,129
Behind it a big mirror,

120
00:03:58,129 --> 00:04:00,737
so you could watch yourself drinking a

121
00:04:00,737 --> 00:04:02,728
soda and see the boys come in

122
00:04:02,728 --> 00:04:05,061
behind you without turning around.

123
00:04:05,241 --> 00:04:07,796
Linda said that mirror was the most

124
00:04:07,796 --> 00:04:10,059
important invention in history.

125
00:04:10,410 --> 00:04:12,882
Behind the counter stood the soda jerk.

126
00:04:13,062 --> 00:04:15,274
That's what they called the boy who

127
00:04:15,274 --> 00:04:16,284
made the drinks,

128
00:04:16,284 --> 00:04:18,622
because he jerked the tap handle back

129
00:04:18,622 --> 00:04:20,075
to pour the soda water.

130
00:04:20,255 --> 00:04:22,135
Ours was Bobby Shaw,

131
00:04:22,135 --> 00:04:24,862
sixteen, with a white jacket,

132
00:04:24,862 --> 00:04:26,555
a white paper hat,

133
00:04:26,555 --> 00:04:27,871
and a bow tie.

134
00:04:28,051 --> 00:04:30,255
He could slide a glass down twenty

135
00:04:30,255 --> 00:04:32,200
feet of counter and stop it in

136
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:34,599
front of you without spilling a drop.

137
00:04:34,779 --> 00:04:37,300
Every girl in the ninth grade was

138
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:39,133
in love with Bobby Shaw.

139
00:04:39,483 --> 00:04:41,224
Now, the menu.

140
00:04:41,404 --> 00:04:44,036
A Coca-Cola from the fountain was a

141
00:04:44,036 --> 00:04:44,562
nickel.

142
00:04:44,742 --> 00:04:46,541
Bobby made it right there,

143
00:04:46,541 --> 00:04:48,616
a squirt of syrup in the glass

144
00:04:48,616 --> 00:04:50,969
and then the fizzy soda water from

145
00:04:50,969 --> 00:04:51,522
the tap.

146
00:04:51,702 --> 00:04:53,798
You could ask for a cherry Coke,

147
00:04:53,798 --> 00:04:54,976
or a vanilla Coke,

148
00:04:54,976 --> 00:04:57,203
and he'd add a squirt of something

149
00:04:57,203 --> 00:04:57,530
else.

150
00:04:57,710 --> 00:04:59,138
Or a lime rickey.

151
00:04:59,318 --> 00:05:00,444
Or a phosphate,

152
00:05:00,444 --> 00:05:02,994
which was soda water with a little

153
00:05:02,994 --> 00:05:03,820
acid in it,

154
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:04,945
sour and fizzy,

155
00:05:04,945 --> 00:05:07,870
that nobody under eighty has ever heard

156
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:08,096
of.

157
00:05:08,446 --> 00:05:11,673
A milkshake was twenty or twenty-five cents.

158
00:05:11,853 --> 00:05:13,935
Bobby made it in a tall steel

159
00:05:13,935 --> 00:05:16,447
cup on a green Hamilton Beach mixer

160
00:05:16,447 --> 00:05:18,529
that whined like an airplane.

161
00:05:18,709 --> 00:05:21,073
A malted was a milkshake with a

162
00:05:21,073 --> 00:05:23,437
spoonful of malted milk powder,

163
00:05:23,437 --> 00:05:25,419
and it tasted like heaven.

164
00:05:25,599 --> 00:05:27,819
And he'd give you the glass and

165
00:05:27,819 --> 00:05:29,108
the steel cup too,

166
00:05:29,108 --> 00:05:30,899
with the leftovers in it,

167
00:05:30,899 --> 00:05:33,262
so you really got two milkshakes.

168
00:05:33,442 --> 00:05:35,774
Two milkshakes for a quarter,

169
00:05:35,774 --> 00:05:36,658
sweetheart.

170
00:05:37,008 --> 00:05:38,724
And the banana split,

171
00:05:38,724 --> 00:05:40,523
for thirty-five cents,

172
00:05:40,523 --> 00:05:42,813
if somebody else was paying.

173
00:05:42,993 --> 00:05:44,676
Three scoops, vanilla,

174
00:05:44,676 --> 00:05:46,588
chocolate and strawberry,

175
00:05:46,588 --> 00:05:48,577
a banana cut the long way,

176
00:05:48,577 --> 00:05:50,643
chocolate sauce, pineapple,

177
00:05:50,643 --> 00:05:52,785
strawberries, whipped cream,

178
00:05:52,785 --> 00:05:55,310
chopped nuts, and a cherry on top

179
00:05:55,310 --> 00:05:56,228
of each one.

180
00:05:56,408 --> 00:05:59,496
Linda and I split one every Friday.

181
00:05:59,676 --> 00:06:02,247
That's why it's called a split,

182
00:06:02,247 --> 00:06:03,159
Linda said.

183
00:06:03,339 --> 00:06:04,363
She was wrong,

184
00:06:04,363 --> 00:06:06,044
but I let her think so.

185
00:06:06,395 --> 00:06:08,577
And the ice cream soda,

186
00:06:08,577 --> 00:06:09,146
honey.

187
00:06:09,326 --> 00:06:11,434
That was the real queen of the

188
00:06:11,434 --> 00:06:12,066
fountain.

189
00:06:12,246 --> 00:06:15,006
Bobby put a squirt of chocolate syrup

190
00:06:15,006 --> 00:06:16,200
in a tall glass,

191
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:17,468
a splash of milk,

192
00:06:17,468 --> 00:06:19,259
soda water from the tap,

193
00:06:19,259 --> 00:06:21,796
and then two scoops of vanilla ice

194
00:06:21,796 --> 00:06:23,437
cream floating on top,

195
00:06:23,437 --> 00:06:24,854
with whipped cream.

196
00:06:25,034 --> 00:06:26,451
Fifteen cents.

197
00:06:26,631 --> 00:06:28,504
You ate it with a long spoon

198
00:06:28,504 --> 00:06:30,846
and drank it through a paper straw,

199
00:06:30,846 --> 00:06:33,121
and the bubbles went up your nose.

200
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:35,836
My mother had one every Saturday after

201
00:06:35,836 --> 00:06:37,237
the grocery shopping.

202
00:06:37,417 --> 00:06:39,539
It was the only thing she ever

203
00:06:39,539 --> 00:06:41,236
bought just for herself.

204
00:06:41,587 --> 00:06:44,207
And Grandma Pearl liked a root beer

205
00:06:44,207 --> 00:06:46,902
float, which she called a black cow,

206
00:06:46,902 --> 00:06:49,073
the way they did when she was

207
00:06:49,073 --> 00:06:49,597
a girl.

208
00:06:49,777 --> 00:06:51,461
She sat at the end of the

209
00:06:51,461 --> 00:06:54,694
counter on Saturday mornings with her pocketbook

210
00:06:54,694 --> 00:06:56,715
in her lap and told Bobby Shaw

211
00:06:56,715 --> 00:06:57,928
he was too skinny.

212
00:06:58,108 --> 00:07:00,752
He'd give her an extra scoop and

213
00:07:00,752 --> 00:07:02,322
say, don't tell Mr.

214
00:07:02,502 --> 00:07:04,360
Hoffman, Mrs.

215
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:05,178
Miller.

216
00:07:05,358 --> 00:07:06,531
She never did.

217
00:07:06,881 --> 00:07:09,554
At noon, the counter was full of

218
00:07:09,554 --> 00:07:10,305
grownups.

219
00:07:10,485 --> 00:07:12,877
Men from the bank and the stores,

220
00:07:12,877 --> 00:07:14,398
ladies from shopping,

221
00:07:14,398 --> 00:07:16,790
eating lunch on those red stools.

222
00:07:16,970 --> 00:07:20,078
A grilled cheese sandwich was about thirty

223
00:07:20,078 --> 00:07:20,522
cents.

224
00:07:20,702 --> 00:07:22,873
A BLT, forty.

225
00:07:23,053 --> 00:07:24,493
A bowl of chili.

226
00:07:24,673 --> 00:07:27,192
A slice of pie under a glass

227
00:07:27,192 --> 00:07:28,812
dome, with coffee,

228
00:07:28,812 --> 00:07:29,712
a quarter.

229
00:07:29,892 --> 00:07:32,090
The coffee came in a heavy white

230
00:07:32,090 --> 00:07:34,700
cup, and Bobby filled it again without

231
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:37,104
asking until you put your hand over

232
00:07:37,104 --> 00:07:37,311
it.

233
00:07:37,661 --> 00:07:40,477
And sweetheart, I have to tell you

234
00:07:40,477 --> 00:07:41,968
the hard part too.

235
00:07:42,148 --> 00:07:43,855
In a lot of the country,

236
00:07:43,855 --> 00:07:45,561
in nineteen fifty-seven,

237
00:07:45,561 --> 00:07:48,050
not everybody was allowed to sit at

238
00:07:48,050 --> 00:07:48,975
that counter.

239
00:07:49,155 --> 00:07:50,103
In the South,

240
00:07:50,103 --> 00:07:53,240
Black customers couldn't be served there at

241
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:53,531
all.

242
00:07:53,711 --> 00:07:55,009
Three years later,

243
00:07:55,009 --> 00:07:56,307
in nineteen sixty,

244
00:07:56,307 --> 00:07:59,119
four young college students sat down at

245
00:07:59,119 --> 00:08:02,147
a Woolworth's lunch counter in Greensboro,

246
00:08:02,147 --> 00:08:05,175
North Carolina, and refused to leave until

247
00:08:05,175 --> 00:08:06,401
they were served.

248
00:08:06,581 --> 00:08:09,318
They changed the whole country from four

249
00:08:09,318 --> 00:08:09,797
stools.

250
00:08:10,148 --> 00:08:12,558
In the corner was the jukebox,

251
00:08:12,558 --> 00:08:14,968
a Wurlitzer, all lit up in red

252
00:08:14,968 --> 00:08:17,218
and gold and bubbling tubes.

253
00:08:17,398 --> 00:08:18,742
A nickel a song,

254
00:08:18,742 --> 00:08:20,254
six for a quarter.

255
00:08:20,434 --> 00:08:22,376
That year it was Elvis,

256
00:08:22,376 --> 00:08:25,078
All Shook Up and Jailhouse Rock.

257
00:08:25,258 --> 00:08:26,574
And Buddy Holly,

258
00:08:26,574 --> 00:08:28,137
That'll Be the Day.

259
00:08:28,317 --> 00:08:30,187
And Paul Anka,

260
00:08:30,187 --> 00:08:30,988
Diana.

261
00:08:31,168 --> 00:08:33,849
Danny would put in a nickel for

262
00:08:33,849 --> 00:08:36,531
Elvis and play it over and over

263
00:08:36,531 --> 00:08:37,309
until Mr.

264
00:08:37,489 --> 00:08:39,826
Hoffman came out from the back and

265
00:08:39,826 --> 00:08:40,926
pulled the plug.

266
00:08:41,276 --> 00:08:42,514
In the evenings,

267
00:08:42,514 --> 00:08:44,062
when school was out,

268
00:08:44,062 --> 00:08:46,539
the booths in the back were full

269
00:08:46,539 --> 00:08:47,545
of teenagers.

270
00:08:47,725 --> 00:08:51,510
Letter jackets and ponytails and saddle shoes.

271
00:08:51,690 --> 00:08:53,914
That's where you found out who was

272
00:08:53,914 --> 00:08:56,008
going steady, who had broken up,

273
00:08:56,008 --> 00:08:57,905
who was going to the sock hop

274
00:08:57,905 --> 00:08:58,691
on Saturday.

275
00:08:58,871 --> 00:09:02,072
It was our telephone and our newspaper

276
00:09:02,072 --> 00:09:04,600
and our television all in one,

277
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:05,105
honey.

278
00:09:05,285 --> 00:09:07,675
And it all happened over a nickel

279
00:09:07,675 --> 00:09:08,037
Coke.

280
00:09:08,387 --> 00:09:10,686
And that's where I had my first

281
00:09:10,686 --> 00:09:11,057
date.

282
00:09:11,237 --> 00:09:12,618
Well, sort of.

283
00:09:12,798 --> 00:09:14,973
A boy named Gary Lutz from my

284
00:09:14,973 --> 00:09:17,073
class asked me if I wanted a

285
00:09:17,073 --> 00:09:18,423
Coke after school,

286
00:09:18,423 --> 00:09:19,323
and he paid.

287
00:09:19,503 --> 00:09:20,745
A whole nickel.

288
00:09:20,925 --> 00:09:23,059
We sat at the counter and didn't

289
00:09:23,059 --> 00:09:25,059
say one word to each other for

290
00:09:25,059 --> 00:09:27,727
ten minutes, staring at ourselves in the

291
00:09:27,727 --> 00:09:28,193
mirror.

292
00:09:28,373 --> 00:09:29,845
Then he said,

293
00:09:29,845 --> 00:09:31,430
well, see you,

294
00:09:31,430 --> 00:09:32,448
and left.

295
00:09:32,628 --> 00:09:34,440
Linda said it counted.

296
00:09:34,620 --> 00:09:36,210
I'm not sure it did.

297
00:09:36,561 --> 00:09:39,181
In the fall of nineteen fifty-eight,

298
00:09:39,181 --> 00:09:41,146
Bobby Shaw joined the Army.

299
00:09:41,326 --> 00:09:42,561
On his last day,

300
00:09:42,561 --> 00:09:44,952
he made every girl in the store

301
00:09:44,952 --> 00:09:46,417
a free cherry Coke,

302
00:09:46,417 --> 00:09:46,957
and Mr.

303
00:09:47,137 --> 00:09:49,750
Hoffman pretended not to see.

304
00:09:49,930 --> 00:09:51,996
Linda cried into hers.

305
00:09:52,176 --> 00:09:54,145
Then a new soda jerk came,

306
00:09:54,145 --> 00:09:56,644
a boy named Ronnie with big ears,

307
00:09:56,644 --> 00:09:59,068
and within a month every girl in

308
00:09:59,068 --> 00:10:01,491
the ninth grade was in love with

309
00:10:01,491 --> 00:10:02,021
Ronnie.

310
00:10:02,201 --> 00:10:03,607
That's how it was,

311
00:10:03,607 --> 00:10:04,465
sweetheart.

312
00:10:04,645 --> 00:10:07,583
The stool never stayed empty for long.

313
00:10:07,933 --> 00:10:09,356
There were rules,

314
00:10:09,356 --> 00:10:10,278
sweetheart.

315
00:10:10,458 --> 00:10:12,524
No feet on the stools.

316
00:10:12,704 --> 00:10:15,232
No chewing gum stuck under the counter,

317
00:10:15,232 --> 00:10:16,594
though everybody did.

318
00:10:16,774 --> 00:10:18,724
Boys took their caps off.

319
00:10:18,904 --> 00:10:20,809
If you were loud,

320
00:10:20,809 --> 00:10:21,145
Mr.

321
00:10:21,325 --> 00:10:23,527
Hoffman would look at you over his

322
00:10:23,527 --> 00:10:24,952
glasses from the back,

323
00:10:24,952 --> 00:10:26,248
and that was enough.

324
00:10:26,428 --> 00:10:28,670
And if you didn't have the nickel,

325
00:10:28,670 --> 00:10:30,714
Bobby would give you a glass of

326
00:10:30,714 --> 00:10:32,429
water with a cherry in it,

327
00:10:32,429 --> 00:10:34,342
and call it a Shirley Temple,

328
00:10:34,342 --> 00:10:35,924
so you didn't feel poor.

329
00:10:36,275 --> 00:10:37,912
So what happened to it?

330
00:10:38,092 --> 00:10:39,532
Little by little,

331
00:10:39,532 --> 00:10:40,634
it went away.

332
00:10:40,814 --> 00:10:43,403
In nineteen fifty-five a man named Ray

333
00:10:43,403 --> 00:10:45,924
Kroc opened a McDonald's in Illinois,

334
00:10:45,924 --> 00:10:49,603
fifteen-cent hamburgers, and soon there were drive-ins

335
00:10:49,603 --> 00:10:52,192
and burger stands out by the highways.

336
00:10:52,372 --> 00:10:55,008
Then the big chain drugstores came,

337
00:10:55,008 --> 00:10:57,794
and they didn't want a soda fountain.

338
00:10:57,974 --> 00:10:59,022
Too much work,

339
00:10:59,022 --> 00:11:00,296
too little money.

340
00:11:00,476 --> 00:11:02,871
They pulled the counters out and put

341
00:11:02,871 --> 00:11:03,936
in more shelves.

342
00:11:04,286 --> 00:11:07,783
Hoffman's closed in nineteen seventy-one,

343
00:11:07,783 --> 00:11:08,465
when Mr.

344
00:11:08,645 --> 00:11:10,271
Hoffman retired.

345
00:11:10,451 --> 00:11:12,620
They took out the marble counter and

346
00:11:12,620 --> 00:11:14,669
the stools and the mirror and sold

347
00:11:14,669 --> 00:11:15,814
them at an auction.

348
00:11:15,994 --> 00:11:18,115
I heard a man from Columbus bought

349
00:11:18,115 --> 00:11:19,860
the mirror for his basement.

350
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:21,315
Somewhere in Ohio,

351
00:11:21,315 --> 00:11:24,075
there's a mirror that has watched every

352
00:11:24,075 --> 00:11:26,553
girl in Mansfield fall in love with

353
00:11:26,553 --> 00:11:27,332
Bobby Shaw.

354
00:11:27,512 --> 00:11:29,717
I hope it's somewhere nice.

355
00:11:30,068 --> 00:11:32,854
There are still a few soda fountains

356
00:11:32,854 --> 00:11:34,092
left in America,

357
00:11:34,092 --> 00:11:34,944
sweetheart.

358
00:11:35,124 --> 00:11:37,957
Old ones, kept by people who love

359
00:11:37,957 --> 00:11:38,386
them.

360
00:11:38,566 --> 00:11:40,638
If you ever see one,

361
00:11:40,638 --> 00:11:41,260
go in.

362
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:44,231
Sit on the stool and spin around

363
00:11:44,231 --> 00:11:44,667
once.

364
00:11:44,847 --> 00:11:47,217
Order a cherry Coke from the fountain

365
00:11:47,217 --> 00:11:48,690
and watch them make it.

366
00:11:48,870 --> 00:11:50,658
And look in the mirror.

367
00:11:50,838 --> 00:11:52,618
You might see me there,

368
00:11:52,618 --> 00:11:54,089
thirteen years old,

369
00:11:54,089 --> 00:11:56,720
waiting for Bobby Shaw to slide me

370
00:11:56,720 --> 00:11:57,340
a glass.

371
00:11:57,690 --> 00:11:59,895
Now tell me in the comments.

372
00:12:00,075 --> 00:12:02,444
What was the name of the drugstore

373
00:12:02,444 --> 00:12:03,349
in your town,

374
00:12:03,349 --> 00:12:04,952
and what did you order?

375
00:12:05,132 --> 00:12:08,905
I'll bet somebody out there remembers phosphates.

376
00:12:09,085 --> 00:12:10,931
Goodnight, sweetheart.

377
00:12:11,111 --> 00:12:13,859
Grandma Ruth will be right here with

378
00:12:13,859 --> 00:12:15,616
another story tomorrow.
